// PRR, RFC 9937, spreads the window's reduction over the round trip in which the loss shows. The peer has neither
// timestamps nor SACK, so a segment is 1000 bytes and each duplicate ACK stands for one segment the peer holds. Ten
// segments acknowledged one by one double the window to 20000, a write fills it with twenty, and the first is lost.
// A write of 1000 follows every duplicate and shows what the window allows; the send cap is raised to hold them.
//
// Duplicates 1 and 2 each take 1000 out of what is in flight, and one new segment leaves: limited transmit, 22000
// outstanding. Duplicate 3 marks the head lost and recovery begins. The target leaves out the two segments sent
// beyond the window (RFC 5681 3.2): 20000 / 2 = 10000. RecoverFS is the whole flight, 22000. With 3000 counted
// received and 1000 lost, 18000 are in flight. Each duplicate from here delivers 1000, and while what is in flight is
// above the target, ceil(prr_delivered * 10000 / 22000) may have been sent in recovery in all: 455 after duplicate
// 3, 910 after 4, 1364 after 5, 1819 after 6 and so on, 5 / 11 of what was delivered, rounded up.
//   3: 455 allowed, nothing sent yet: a window of 18455, on which the head is resent whole; a write gets nothing.
//   4: 910 allowed, 1000 sent: nothing.
//   5: 1364 allowed: a write sends 364.
//   6 to 17: 454 or 455 more at each, as the rounding falls, and a write sends it.
//   18: 9819 in flight, below the target: the conservative bound lets it up to the target and no further, 181.
//   19: 9000 in flight: a whole segment.
// The ACK of everything ends recovery with the window at the target, so a write accepts 10000.
//
// RFC 9937's Figure 1 is this recovery drawn in whole segments, a target of 10 for 20 in flight: one new segment for
// every second duplicate. In bytes writable() offers what the window leaves, a part of a segment too, so a little
// under half a segment leaves at every duplicate; under half, because the figure's sender reads the peer's blocks,
// which makes its RecoverFS 20 where a count of duplicates leaves 22. Linux counts whole segments, as the figure
// does; its reno halves the window too, 10 segments, and its PRR divides by the window it had on entry, 20, where
// RFC 9937 divides by RecoverFS.
--congestion=newreno
--tcp_send_segments=64
0 socket(..., SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP) = 3
+0 bind(3, ..., ...) = 0
+0 listen(3, 1) = 0

+0 < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1000>
+0 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
+0 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 65535
+0 accept(3, ..., ...) = 4

+0 write(4, ..., 20000) = 10000
